    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Two Sum\n\nGiven an array of integers nums and an integer target, return the indices of the two numbers that add up to target. Exactly one solution exists, and you may not use the same element twice. Return the answer in any order.\n\nExample: nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9 -> [0,1]\nConstraints: 2 <= len(nums) <= 10^4, -10^9 <= nums[i], target <= 10^9.",
      "id": "m-1",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-02T21:02:53.499Z",
      "durationMs": 0
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "channel": "analysis",
      "content": "Writing the solution, then tracing it on the first example to confirm indices are handled correctly.\n\nEdge cases to cover before submitting: empty input, single element, and the all-duplicates case.\n\nComplexity check: one pass, O(n) time, O(n) auxiliary space for the map. That meets the constraint.\n\nConstraints are small enough that the obvious approach fits, but the follow-up asks for the optimal one; going straight to it.",
